"""Publish the completed native longitudinal study as a local research report."""
import argparse,json,html,shutil
from pathlib import Path
p=argparse.ArgumentParser();p.add_argument('--runs',type=Path,required=True);p.add_argument('--output',type=Path,required=True);a=p.parse_args();a.output.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
m=json.loads((a.runs/'metrics.json').read_text());sections=[]
def table(cases,phases):
 rows=['<table><tr><th>Case / phase</th><th>Height RMSE m</th><th>Pitch-rate SD deg/s</th><th>Peak |q| deg/s</th><th>Turn-rate RMSE deg/s</th><th>Mean throttle %</th><th>Throttle at limits %</th></tr>']
 for case in cases:
  if case not in m:continue
  for phase in phases:
   d=m[case][phase];values=[d['height_rmse'],d['q_std'],d['q_peak_abs'],d['turn_error_rms'],100*d['throttle_mean'],100*d['throttle_clip_fraction']]
   rows.append('<tr><td>'+html.escape(case+' / '+phase)+'</td>'+''.join(f'<td>{v:.3f}</td>' for v in values)+'</tr>')
 return ''.join(rows)+'</table>'
def figure(source,name,caption):
 if not source.exists():return ''
 shutil.copy2(source,a.output/name)
 return f'<figure><img src="{name}"><figcaption>{caption}</figcaption></figure>'
sections.append('''<h1>Mission longitudinal controller: two focused revisions</h1><p>10 October 2026 · native Plane/TECS · calibrated Trout Lake two-body model</p><p><strong>Selected candidate: damper off, calibrated feedforward, and 0.5-second smoothing of altitude-rate feedforward.</strong> The useful improvements came from eliminating excessive lateral brake feedback, matching steering and vertical feedforward to the simulator, and reducing altitude-capture throttle kicks. Neither tested pitch damper added enough benefit to select.</p><h2>What changed</h2><p>Revision one reduced PG_TURN_P from 0.04 to 0.005 and RDAMP from 0.03 to 0.003 while retaining the original 4 Hz filter, integral gain and rate limit. Cruise throttle became 45% and climb calibration 1.0 m/s, consistent with level trim at 44.59% and +1 m/s trim at 59.91% under the 60% throttle ceiling. Holding brakes fixed isolated this longitudinal benefit: climb height RMSE improved from 0.728 to about 0.080 m.</p><p>Revision two corrected steering feedforward from 0.051 to 0.009 and disabled acceleration feedforward. The model's left/right 10 deg/s trims imply different feedforward values, approximately 0.00564 and 0.01229 including neutral bias; the existing integral term handles their asymmetry. Sink calibration became 2.5 m/s to fit the local descending-throttle slope. This parameter is not a newly validated full-glide sink limit.</p><p>The native implementation adds TECS_PG_HR_TC, a first-order smoother on altitude-trajectory derivative feedforward. At 0.5 s it reduces the brief throttle kick when FBWB captures current altitude. Position feedback and altitude slew limits remain unchanged. It defaults to zero, preserving previous behavior. An optional pitch-rate washout parameter TECS_PG_PR_WASH was also implemented and tested, but remains disabled in the selected candidate.</p><h2>Matched nominal performance</h2>''')
sections.append(table(['baseline_matched','r2_off'],['climb','climb_capture','descent','right_turn','left_turn']))
sections.append(figure(a.runs/'final-comparison.png','final-comparison.png','Matched native baseline versus selected candidate, phase aligned.'))
sections.append(figure(a.runs/'r2_off/response.png','candidate-response.png','Full selected-candidate response. The actual altitude-rate ramps are approximately ±0.3 m/s after native RC mapping and altitude-command discretization.'))
sections.append('''<h2>Capture smoothing: benefit and cost</h2><p>The matched revision-two ablation reduces peak pitch rate during climb capture from 64.06 to 14.74 deg/s, a 77% reduction. Climb height RMSE increases from about 0.10 to 0.14 m: a small tracking-lag cost for a much gentler transition. This is a retained tradeoff, not an attempt to make every score improve.</p>''')
sections.append(table(['r2_raw_feedforward','r2_off'],['climb','climb_capture']))
sections.append(figure(a.runs/'capture-smoothing.png','capture-smoothing.png','Only the altitude-rate feedforward smoothing setting differs.'))
sections.append('''<h2>Why no pitch damper was selected</h2><p>Each revision compared off, reversed K=−0.02 with the original 10 Hz filter, and reversed K=−0.02 with 0.6 Hz washout and 3 Hz low-pass. Feedback is Δthrottle=−Kq. In revision one, excessive steering feedforward still drove an over-commanded turn; both damper variants worsened its slow motion. After steering calibration, all three final alternatives track similarly, but the damper-off candidate generally has less steady pitch-rate variation. The filtered alternative reduces a few transient peaks without a convincing mission-level benefit.</p>''')
sections.append(table(['r2_off','r2_reversed','r2_shaped'],['climb','right_turn','left_turn']))
sections.append(figure(a.runs/'final-dampers.png','final-dampers.png','Final damping alternatives, with other gains held constant.'))
sections.append('<h2>Uncertainty checks without retuning</h2><p>The candidate is repeated with the softer dynamic-roll profile and separately at 20.4 V instead of 22.2 V. These are model sensitivity checks, not flight validation. At 20.4 V, climb height RMSE increases from 0.14 to 0.48 m and the capture transient grows, without throttle saturation. Voltage-aware feedforward is a useful next investigation; this study retains the same gains.</p>')
sections.append(table(['r2_off','candidate_dynamic_roll','candidate_low_voltage'],['climb','descent','right_turn','left_turn']))
for case in ['candidate_dynamic_roll','candidate_low_voltage']:sections.append(figure(a.runs/case/'response.png',case+'.png',case+' — same selected controller settings.'))
if (a.runs/'auto-comparison/summary.json').exists():
 auto=json.loads((a.runs/'auto-comparison/summary.json').read_text())
 execution=(a.runs/'candidate_auto.log').read_text(errors='replace')
 passed='PASSED STEP: test.Plane.ParagliderAutoMission' in execution
 sections.append('<p><strong>AUTO execution: '+('PASSED' if passed else 'NOT CONFIRMED PASSED')+'</strong></p>')
 sections.append('<h2>Extended AUTO route</h2><p>The existing mission exercises altitude changes and opposing two-turn loiters, with its original acceptance checks unchanged. See the execution result and numerical summary below; route completion alone is not a sufficient stability measure. The candidate completed the 1,200-second mission record with maximum waypoint altitude error 1.67 m and corner overshoot 0.66 m. First-loiter bank standard deviation fell from 2.60 degrees with the high-DOF original gains to 0.039 degrees; median loiter radii were 26.74 and 26.68 m for the 25 m requests. These gains trade slightly larger radius and corner errors for much steadier motion.</p><pre>'+html.escape(json.dumps(auto.get('Longitudinal mission candidate',{}),indent=2))+'</pre>')
 sections.append(figure(a.runs/'auto-comparison/comparison.png','auto-comparison.png','Historical/native AUTO baselines and the selected candidate.'))
 sections.append(figure(a.runs/'auto-comparison/loiter-detail.png','auto-loiter.png','Last 30 seconds of the first loiter: bank variation and radius.'))
sections.append('''<h2>Method and scope</h2><p>Three initial baseline/ablation runs, five first-revision runs, four second-revision runs, then two sensitivity checks and the extended AUTO check. The exploratory baseline had climb/descent labels reversed and is excluded from matched comparisons; baseline_matched uses corrected RC direction and the rebuilt firmware. The final raw-feedforward ablation is part of revision two, not a third design revision. Model coefficients were not tuned during this study.</p><p>Phase height error uses TECS's estimated height minus logged filtered height demand. Climb tracking metrics reconstruct the unfiltered trajectory derivative from TECS.hdem so feedforward filtering does not silently smooth the scoring reference. Pitch-rate standard deviation, spectral RMS and turn-rate tracking use the final 20 s; height error, throttle statistics and pitch-rate peaks use each complete phase. Body pitch rate has a nonzero mean in banked turns, so total RMS alone is not interpreted as oscillation. Plots include independent plant-truth climb and attitude.</p><p>The demonstrated envelope is powered up-and-away flight at 1.758 kg, modest climb/descent ramps, sustained turns, and the reported AUTO route/model sensitivity cases. Launch, landing, canopy collapse, wind robustness, full-throttle climb, maximum glide sink, battery depletion and flight deployment are outside this study. No claim is made that simulated amplitudes exactly reproduce Hood River.</p><h2>Reproduction and files</h2><p>Use Tools/simulation/paraglider/LONGITUDINAL-STUDY.md in the active ArduPilot branch. mission-controller-candidate.json and .parm contain the selected settings. PG_CONTROLLER_CONFIG loads the JSON into the airborne AUTO comparison harness after baseline settings. Native code defaults retain the previous behavior; the candidate explicitly opts into smoothing and leaves pitch damping off.</p><p>Raw runs remain under /home/david/repos/pg-longitudinal-design. Mission Planner retains this report and the research log. Only final report-linked figures and plotting source are archived in ArduPilot-tinkering/research/flight-log-analysis.</p><p><a href="metrics.json">All phase metrics</a> · <a href="mission-controller-candidate.json">Selected configuration</a></p>''')
shutil.copy2(a.runs/'metrics.json',a.output/'metrics.json');shutil.copy2(Path(__file__).with_name('mission-controller-candidate.json'),a.output/'mission-controller-candidate.json')
head='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Mission longitudinal controller study</title><style>body{font:17px/1.5 system-ui;max-width:1200px;margin:36px auto;padding:0 20px;color:#17313d}img{width:100%}table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px;width:100%}td,th{padding:7px;border-bottom:1px solid #ccd;text-align:right}td:first-child,th:first-child{text-align:left}pre{overflow:auto;background:#edf3f5;padding:20px}</style>'
(a.output/'report.html').write_text(head+''.join(sections)+'</html>')
